00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.790
RYAN PRZYBYL: Thanks, Stephanie.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:53.300
As Stephanie said, last time
we covered VPC, shared VPC,

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.320
and interconnect.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.960
Today, we're going to
cover routing in the GCP

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:00.000
environment and VPC peering.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.500
So when we talk about routing,
there's a whole bunch of ways

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.900
that routes get propagated
within the Google Cloud

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:05.572
Platform.

00:01:05.572 --> 00:01:07.405
There are what we call
preprogrammed routes.

00:01:07.405 --> 00:01:09.480
There is dynamic
routes, and there are

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:10.870
what we call advanced routes.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.090
So I'm going to go
through each of those.

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:17.140
Preprogramed routes are what
we have when you set up a VPC,

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:18.100
and you create subnets.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.599
These are routes
that get programmed

00:01:19.599 --> 00:01:23.440
into allow VMs to talk to
other VMs within the VPC.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.510
These are things that are
automatically created.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:28.995
And these are also things that
you cannot delete or change.

00:01:28.995 --> 00:01:30.370
Like I said, these
are the routes

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:33.580
that really allow a VM within
the VPC to talk to other VMs

00:01:33.580 --> 00:01:35.570
within the VPC.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.932
There is also default
routes that get created.

00:01:37.932 --> 00:01:39.890
So the standard default
route that gets created

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:42.290
is going to point to
an internet gateway.

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.354
And a common question
that we get is, hey,

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:45.770
I have this default
route pointing

00:01:45.770 --> 00:01:46.728
to an internet gateway.

00:01:46.728 --> 00:01:48.870
Can my VMs talk to the internet?

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.100
So the answer to that is no,
not unless you put a public IP

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.120
address on that VM.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:56.950
The difference between
the default routes

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.990
and the preprogrammed
routes is default routes

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:00.400
can actually be changed.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.096
Like I said, the
default route that

00:02:02.096 --> 00:02:03.970
will be created within
your cloud environment

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:05.595
actually points to
an internet gateway.

00:02:05.595 --> 00:02:08.289
But let's say, for example, you
want the default route to point

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:10.100
back to your
on-prem environment.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.120
Unlike the preprogrammed routes
for VM to VM communication,

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:15.940
you can actually go in and
change the default route.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:21.960
A big part of understanding
dynamic routing

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.626
within the cloud
environment is actually

00:02:23.626 --> 00:02:25.340
understanding the Cloud Router.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.930
A Cloud Router is,
in a lot of ways,

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.610
very similar to a
traditional router

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:31.110
that you would have in
your on-prem environment.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.901
But there are some
fundamental differences,

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:35.910
which I want to make sure
you guys really understand.

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.460
So in a typical data
center environment,

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:40.860
your router is going to be
a big piece of sheet metal.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.170
It has dual power supplies.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.420
It performs two real functions.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.150
It provides control
plane functionality,

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:51.080
which is really the BGP route
communication mechanisms.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.460
And it also provides
data point functionality.

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.252
That is packets go
into the router.

00:02:56.252 --> 00:02:57.960
The router will look
up its RIBs and FIBs

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:01.400
and make routing decisions
and forward appropriately.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.470
In the cloud environment,
the Cloud Router

00:03:03.470 --> 00:03:06.827
is actually only a
control point device.

00:03:06.827 --> 00:03:08.660
So packets that go into
your router on-prem,

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:10.380
they actually don't
go into the Cloud

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.274
Router in the GCP environment.

00:03:12.274 --> 00:03:14.690
The Cloud Router functions as
a control plane only device.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.450
And really, it learns routes
from your on-prem router

00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:20.150
or from other routers in
the cloud environment.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.190
You can really think about
it as a BGP speaker, right?

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:25.010
It only speaks BGP,
whereas your routers

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:27.590
that you may have on-prem may
speak other routing protocols.

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:28.730
This only speaks BGP.

00:03:28.730 --> 00:03:31.840
So I want to make sure that
we're clear on that front.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.850
What it does is it learns
routes dynamically.

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:35.560
It will configure
routing tables.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.059
But then what it
does is it actually

00:03:37.059 --> 00:03:39.740
pushes those routing tables
down to the VMs themselves.

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:42.790
So you can think of every VM in
a cloud environment as really

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:46.587
host routing, as opposed to,
say, a host in your data center

00:03:46.587 --> 00:03:48.170
that, when it needs
to route a packet,

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:49.910
is going to push the
packet to the router.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.100
The router's going to
look at RIBs and FIBs

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:52.420
and make a forwarding decision.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:54.010
In this case, the
route table actually

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:56.000
lives on the VM itself.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:58.090
So when the VM needs to
communicate to something,

00:03:58.090 --> 00:04:01.730
be it another VM or maybe
your router that is on-prem,

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:04.210
it's actually going to route
directly to that IP address.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:08.084
Because it has that routing
table on the VM itself.

00:04:08.084 --> 00:04:10.250
One of the last things to
understand about the Cloud

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.890
Router is that it is a
Google-managed process.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:14.930
So while you control
it and configure it,

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.172
it's actually a process
that we run on Google hosts.

00:04:17.172 --> 00:04:18.880
And the big part to
understand about this

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.670
is if it fails for
any reason, Google

00:04:21.670 --> 00:04:23.920
will re-spin up
that Cloud router

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:25.690
and re-establish
the environment.

00:04:25.690 --> 00:04:27.340
There is nothing
that you have to do

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.740
in terms of your piece of that.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:31.530
As long as you
configured it correctly,

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.238
in the event of failure,
Google will take

00:04:33.238 --> 00:04:35.910
care of bringing it back up.

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.882
So when you're setting
up the Cloud Router

00:04:37.882 --> 00:04:39.340
or when you're
setting up your VPC,

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:42.020
really, there's two routing
modes that you can enable.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.815
So one is what we
call regional routing.

00:04:43.815 --> 00:04:46.680
And the other's what
we call global routing.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.700
So regional routing is where
when you put a Cloud Router

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:52.830
in a particular
region, it will only

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.820
learn subnets in that region.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.120
So for example, if I
have an east, a central,

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.530
and a west region, and I have
regional routing modes enabled,

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.930
the Cloud Router, if
I put it in the west,

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:06.480
will only learn the subnets
that I put in the West Region.

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.470
And it will only advertise those
subnets out to either a VPN

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.356
that you've connected
it to or a dedicated

00:05:13.356 --> 00:05:14.980
interconnect that
you're connecting to.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:16.680
But it's only picking
up those subnets

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.425
that you configured
in the west region.

00:05:18.425 --> 00:05:20.300
STEPHANIE WONG: I have
a couple of questions,

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.720
actually, going back to that.

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.860
The first question is I know
you talked about cloud routing

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.930
being the option
for Google Cloud,

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:31.690
but do we support any kind of
other routers, such as Cisco?

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:33.380
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
we're working on that.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:37.640
The answer really is we
will support other routers

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.320
in the cloud
environment, but I'm

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.028
going to tell you why
I don't necessarily

00:05:41.028 --> 00:05:43.257
think it's a good thing to do.

00:05:43.257 --> 00:05:44.840
So because our Cloud
Router is heavily

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:46.520
integrated with
our platform, we're

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.580
able to take the data
plane off there, right?

00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:52.070
So if you were to go drop
a Cisco, or a Juniper,

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.870
or other sort of cloud
router on the environment,

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:57.080
it's really creating a
single point of failure.

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.450
Because it's operating like
a very traditional router.

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:01.080
So you're creating
a choke point,

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.200
because all the packets have
to flow through that router.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.630
Because it operates very much
like a traditional router

00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:07.156
in your on-prem.

00:06:07.156 --> 00:06:08.280
Packet flows to the router.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.654
The router will look up, make
a routing decision, forward

00:06:10.654 --> 00:06:11.180
things on.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:12.590
Now, what we're doing
is we're working

00:06:12.590 --> 00:06:13.730
to figure out how
do we integrate

00:06:13.730 --> 00:06:16.400
that more with the platform so
that we can sort of eliminate

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.130
that and make the functionality
there more usable for customers

00:06:19.130 --> 00:06:21.620
and not have to create that
choke-point or single point

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:22.220
of failure.

00:06:22.220 --> 00:06:25.460
Because when that does fail,
because it is a data plane

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:27.895
device, your traffic
dies with it.

00:06:27.895 --> 00:06:30.020
But as I talked about, when
the Cloud Router fails,

00:06:30.020 --> 00:06:32.600
because it's only a
control plane device,

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:34.100
the data plane can
still function,

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:36.140
as long as you have some other
caveats, which I'll talk about

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:36.806
in a little bit.

00:06:36.806 --> 00:06:38.239
STEPHANIE WONG: Great, thanks.

00:06:38.239 --> 00:06:40.280
RYAN PRZYBYL: So let's
talk about global routing.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:41.300
So we talked about
regional routing

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:43.670
where the router only
picks up regional subnets.

00:06:43.670 --> 00:06:46.541
The other option to configure
in your VPC is global routing.

00:06:46.541 --> 00:06:48.915
And just to mention again,
you set this at the VPC level,

00:06:48.915 --> 00:06:51.260
but this is actually how
the cloud routers themselves

00:06:51.260 --> 00:06:53.730
will operate within that VPC.

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:57.450
So global routing-- really,
it allows the router

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.620
to pick up all subnets.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:01.080
So back to my example, if
you had an east, a central,

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:03.660
and a west region, and you
had subnets on all of them,

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:05.080
remember when I said
in the regional mode,

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.210
if you put the cloud
router on the west,

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:07.880
it would only pick
up the west subnet?

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:10.020
But if you're in global mode and
the cloud router's in the west,

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.978
it'll actually pick up
the subnets in the west,

00:07:11.978 --> 00:07:14.220
the central, and the east.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:15.530
So this really helps.

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:18.330
It's very powerful in terms of
when you're configuring VPCs,

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:21.390
as we talked about
in the first session.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:24.470
Where you have a
global VPC construct,

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.490
and you have a
shared VPC construct,

00:07:26.490 --> 00:07:28.740
and you don't want to be
putting cloud routers in all

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:29.850
of the regions all
around the world,

00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:32.370
you can actually put cloud
routers in a couple of regions,

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.250
enable global routing, and
have it learning routes

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:36.910
throughout the entire VPC.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:39.414
So it simplifies your
operational environment.

00:07:39.414 --> 00:07:40.830
Now, there are
some caveats, which

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:42.746
I'll talk about when we
get to load balancing,

00:07:42.746 --> 00:07:44.032
which is in a future session.

00:07:44.032 --> 00:07:46.240
Because load balancers tend
to be regional resources.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:47.460
There are some very
specific use cases

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.110
where you'd want to
use a regional routing

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:50.609
versus global
routing, because there

00:07:50.609 --> 00:07:52.690
are such thing as regional
constructs in the VPC

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:53.190
environment.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:55.502
STEPHANIE WONG: I was about
to follow up by asking,

00:07:55.502 --> 00:07:57.960
we'll save it for the next one
as well, but any kind of use

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:00.870
cases for using one over the
other, regional versus global?

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.536
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah,
it really comes down

00:08:02.536 --> 00:08:03.970
to the case for load balancers.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:05.970
When you start to inject
internal load balancers

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.344
in the environment,
then you have

00:08:07.344 --> 00:08:09.360
to be careful of
using global routing.

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.550
Because its a
regional construct,

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:13.259
you could, in essence,
create black holes

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:14.716
where you're trying
to route things

00:08:14.716 --> 00:08:16.117
from one region to the other.

00:08:16.117 --> 00:08:17.700
But because its a
regional construct--

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:18.908
it only lives in one region--

00:08:18.908 --> 00:08:20.820
say you put the load
balancer in the central,

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:22.390
and you're trying to push
something from the west,

00:08:22.390 --> 00:08:24.056
it may actually not
be able to reach it.

00:08:24.056 --> 00:08:25.905
Because it's a
regional construct.

00:08:25.905 --> 00:08:26.910
STEPHANIE WONG: So
this is something

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:28.005
you have to plan in advance.

00:08:28.005 --> 00:08:29.610
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah, you
have to sort of understand

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:31.970
what components of GCP
you're going to be using.

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:35.140
That will influence what sort
of routing you want to use,

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:38.169
whether it's regional or global.

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:40.419
The other thing to talk about
is route priority, here.

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:42.390
So for those that are
familiar with BGP,

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:45.520
route priority is the equivalent
to the BGP metric MED, which

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.194
is multi exit discriminator.

00:08:47.194 --> 00:08:49.110
So when you see route
priority in our console,

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:50.040
just think of MED.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:51.907
That's how it will
manifest itself

00:08:51.907 --> 00:08:53.490
when you go into
your routers on-prem,

00:08:53.490 --> 00:08:57.220
and you look at the
route priorities.

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.300
So the standard default
route priority for all routes

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:01.900
is 1,000.

00:09:01.900 --> 00:09:04.890
So if you don't change anything,
when you go into your router,

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:07.147
and you look at the MED
that your router is seeing,

00:09:07.147 --> 00:09:09.480
it will see all of your subnets
that you're advertising.

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:11.970
It will see a route
priority of 1,000.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:16.790
Now, that will change depending
on the configuration where

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:20.520
your interconnects are versus
where your subnets are.

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:23.390
So for example, let's go back
to the east, west, central sort

00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:24.290
of environment.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:28.220
If I have a subnet in the
west, and I'm advertising that

00:09:28.220 --> 00:09:31.880
through an interconnect in the
east, what will actually happen

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:33.534
is the Cloud Router
knows that that's

00:09:33.534 --> 00:09:35.200
something that lives
in the west region,

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.070
but that interconnect
is in the east region.

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:40.100
So it will artificially
inflate that route priority

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:43.670
by some value, which is based
on the RTT time from where

00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:46.250
the interconnect was built
to where the subnet is built.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:48.487
So for example, if you were
advertising two subnets--

00:09:48.487 --> 00:09:50.695
one on the east, one on the
west-- to an interconnect

00:09:50.695 --> 00:09:53.400
in the east, and you set
the route priority as 1,000

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:55.400
for both of them, and
then went into your router

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:57.500
and looked at the
metric for MED,

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.520
you would see that
the subnet in the east

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:03.020
had a route priority of 1,000,
which is what you configured.

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:04.811
But you'd see your
subnet in the west maybe

00:10:04.811 --> 00:10:06.710
had a route priority of 1,700.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:10.595
And you're asking well, where
is that other 700 coming from?

00:10:10.595 --> 00:10:11.970
It's coming from
the intelligence

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:13.511
that the Google
Cloud Platform has in

00:10:13.511 --> 00:10:16.064
knowing that subnet is
actually in the west.

00:10:16.064 --> 00:10:18.230
So for example, if I had
an interconnect in the west

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:19.970
as well, and it was
advertising those same routes,

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:21.262
I would see the exact opposite.

00:10:21.262 --> 00:10:23.511
The route in the west, when
you looked at your router,

00:10:23.511 --> 00:10:24.890
would have a MED value of 1,000.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:27.320
And the route in the
east would be 1,700.

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.380
So you can use route
priority where this really

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.930
comes into play, in terms
of traffic engineering

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:35.760
and how you want things
to flow in the network.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.397
So a best practice is to always
have multiple interconnects

00:10:38.397 --> 00:10:40.730
or multiple ways to connect
into your cloud environment.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:42.975
So say you have an
interconnect and a VPN,

00:10:42.975 --> 00:10:45.600
you could advertise all of your
routes across the interconnect.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:48.590
And you could advertise all
of your routes across the VPN.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:51.090
And the way that you would
make the VPN less preferred,

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.715
from a routing
standpoint, is you would

00:10:52.715 --> 00:10:54.260
set the route priority higher.

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.690
So higher route
priority or higher MED

00:10:56.690 --> 00:10:59.307
is always the least preferred.

00:10:59.307 --> 00:11:01.140
For example, you could
advertise the default

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:02.602
1,000 out of the interconnect.

00:11:02.602 --> 00:11:05.060
And then you could advertise
all the same routes with, say,

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.200
a 2,000 out of the VPN tunnel.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.725
And that way, when your router
sees those two connections,

00:11:09.725 --> 00:11:11.600
it's always going to
prefer the interconnect.

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:12.990
But in the event that
the interconnect fails,

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:14.670
it's automatically going
to fail over the VPN.

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:15.570
STEPHANIE WONG:
So there's no way

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:17.600
that Google would know
how to reconciliate

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.817
if you had something that
had the same priority?

00:11:19.817 --> 00:11:21.650
RYAN PRZYBYL: Correct,
if you have something

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:23.060
that has the same
priority-- let's say

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:24.260
you have two
interconnects, and they're

00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:26.330
both advertising the same
priority-- what will typically

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:28.760
happen is you're going ECMP
traffic across both of them.

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:30.843
So basically, you are load
balancing that traffic.

00:11:30.843 --> 00:11:33.770
Because it sees two routes
with the same exact priority.

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:35.940
There's no preference
to one or the other.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:37.149
It's going to use both paths.

00:11:37.149 --> 00:11:39.439
So in this case, it could
use an interconnect and a VPN

00:11:39.439 --> 00:11:40.630
or multiple interconnects.

00:11:43.960 --> 00:11:46.240
So why is this stuff
really important to grasp

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:47.820
in terms of the Cloud Router?

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:50.380
I talk about failure impact.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.450
When a cloud router
fails, like I said,

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:54.495
your data plane
can still function.

00:11:54.495 --> 00:11:55.870
You just lost your
control plane.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:11:57.828
The only thing is that
you can't update routes.

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:02.390
But you have to treat that a
little bit differently in terms

00:12:02.390 --> 00:12:04.280
of your router that's on-prem.

00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:07.640
So what you need to enable is
what we call graceful restart.

00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:10.050
Graceful restart-- in
essence, what it is--

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:13.130
your on-prem router-- when it
sees the Cloud Router fails

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:15.642
and sees the BGP session drop,
what it's not going to do

00:12:15.642 --> 00:12:17.600
is it's not going to
start stripping routes out

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:18.734
of the routing table.

00:12:18.734 --> 00:12:20.900
It's going to say, hey, I
saw the Cloud Router fail.

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:23.390
I saw my BGP session fail.

00:12:23.390 --> 00:12:24.980
But I'm just going
to play it cool.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:27.530
I'm not going to mess
with any of the routing.

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:29.060
And what's happening in the
back side, is like I said,

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:30.434
this is a
Google-managed service.

00:12:30.434 --> 00:12:32.870
So while Google is spinning
up that Cloud Router,

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:34.300
you're still pushing packets.

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.310
Because again, it's a control
plane only device on our side.

00:12:37.310 --> 00:12:40.730
So you can still push
data plane packets.

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:42.272
And what happens is
after 60 seconds,

00:12:42.272 --> 00:12:44.021
the Cloud Router is
going to come back up.

00:12:44.021 --> 00:12:46.010
It's going to reestablish
BGP connectivity

00:12:46.010 --> 00:12:47.514
with your router on-prem.

00:12:47.514 --> 00:12:49.430
And then everything is
going to be fine again.

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:50.510
Now, the flip side
is if you didn't

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:52.430
have graceful restart
enabled, as soon

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:53.960
as the Cloud Router
failed, you'd

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:55.550
start tearing routes down.

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:58.520
Even though the data plane
is still up and functioning,

00:12:58.520 --> 00:13:00.320
it would serve no
purpose to really start

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:01.760
tearing those routes down.

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:03.740
So that's a key piece
to really understand

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:04.940
the difference between,
like I talked about,

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.770
Cisco and Juniper or
the typical routers

00:13:06.770 --> 00:13:10.461
that you have on-prem versus
how a Cloud Router works.

00:13:10.461 --> 00:13:12.710
STEPHANIE WONG: We're seeing
a lot of value add here--

00:13:12.710 --> 00:13:14.960
the cloud routing versus
kind of the traditional side,

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:17.624
where that would be more of
a single point of failure.

00:13:17.624 --> 00:13:19.790
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah, and
even with the Cloud Routers,

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:21.590
we recommend running
multiple Cloud Routers

00:13:21.590 --> 00:13:22.880
in the environment.

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:25.520
Our typical practice
is always run two.

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.860
And if you're enabled
with global routing,

00:13:27.860 --> 00:13:29.240
and let's say you have a VPC--

00:13:29.240 --> 00:13:31.379
let's go back to my east,
west, central example--

00:13:31.379 --> 00:13:33.920
you could run one router on the
west, one router in the east,

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:35.322
and have global routing enabled.

00:13:35.322 --> 00:13:36.530
It's learning all the routes.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:38.696
Maybe you have an interconnect
in the east and west,

00:13:38.696 --> 00:13:41.000
you're advertising all routes
across all interconnects.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.880
That way if one Cloud
Router does fail,

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:46.070
you still have connectivity
to another Cloud Router.

00:13:46.070 --> 00:13:48.620
Now again, we're going to reboot
the Cloud Router that failed.

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:49.820
But having two means
you're always going

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:50.910
to have that BGP connectivity.

00:13:50.910 --> 00:13:52.150
You're always going
to able to route.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:53.030
STEPHANIE WONG: So of
course, there's still

00:13:53.030 --> 00:13:55.040
best practices that people
should be following--

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:56.937
setting up multiple
Cloud Routers,

00:13:56.937 --> 00:13:58.520
everything that you
just talked about.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:13:59.640
RYAN PRZYBYL: Typical
best practices

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:02.120
are use multiple Cloud
Routers, use global routing,

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:04.360
unless you have some
complex scenarios with ILBs

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:05.360
or various other things.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.297
Those are two best practices.

00:14:07.297 --> 00:14:09.380
And then on the on-prem
side, like I talked about,

00:14:09.380 --> 00:14:10.380
set up graceful restart.

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:12.200
It's definitely a best practice.

00:14:12.200 --> 00:14:14.032
So that when the
Cloud Router fails,

00:14:14.032 --> 00:14:15.740
you don't start
stripping the routes out.

00:14:15.740 --> 00:14:18.161
The other thing to understand
is a Cloud Router--

00:14:18.161 --> 00:14:20.410
if you think about your
routers that you have on-prem,

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:21.230
it's a big box.

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:26.030
It's designed with very small
mean time between failure sort

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:26.630
of scenarios.

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:28.160
They're designed
never to go down.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:29.540
So we're taking all
that intelligence

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:31.123
that you've put on
a router, and we're

00:14:31.123 --> 00:14:33.290
putting on a very slim
little Google-managed host.

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:35.587
So these are not these
big robust chassis

00:14:35.587 --> 00:14:36.420
that your router is.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:39.584
So it will fail at a higher rate
than, say, an on-prem router.

00:14:39.584 --> 00:14:41.000
Now, that shouldn't
scare anybody,

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:41.900
because I just talked about it.

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:43.108
The router can actually fail.

00:14:43.108 --> 00:14:46.136
It does not disrupt traffic
in any way, shape, or form.

00:14:46.136 --> 00:14:48.260
But you do have to understand
that the construct is

00:14:48.260 --> 00:14:48.600
different.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:49.910
So that's why you have
to have to start planning

00:14:49.910 --> 00:14:51.760
for these failures
and engineer for them.

00:14:51.760 --> 00:14:53.718
So that when they do
happen, it doesn't disrupt

00:14:53.718 --> 00:14:56.309
the actual traffic flow.

00:14:56.309 --> 00:14:58.600
So I talked a little about
using multiple Cloud Routers

00:14:58.600 --> 00:14:59.710
for high availability.

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:01.180
We talked a little about
using route priority

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:02.200
for traffic engineering.

00:15:02.200 --> 00:15:04.690
Again, this is where if you
have multiple interconnects,

00:15:04.690 --> 00:15:07.660
you can be using the route
priority to dictate flow.

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:10.330
If I want to choose
interconnect A versus B,

00:15:10.330 --> 00:15:12.206
a common scenario
customers may have is maybe

00:15:12.206 --> 00:15:14.455
I have some very critical
infrastructure in the cloud.

00:15:14.455 --> 00:15:16.420
I'm going to build a
VPN tunnel, but I'm only

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:19.870
going to advertise that
subnet over that tunnel.

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:21.370
So maybe I'm
advertising that subnet

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:23.744
over the interconnect with a
whole bunch of other subnets

00:15:23.744 --> 00:15:25.150
at a route priority of 1,000.

00:15:25.150 --> 00:15:26.586
And that's my normal path.

00:15:26.586 --> 00:15:28.960
But I'm going to advertise
just that subnet, because it's

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:30.250
my critical subnet,
where I have a lot

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:32.290
of critical infrastructure
over a VPN tunnel.

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:34.720
I'm going to do it with,
say, a MED of 2,000.

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:37.720
So if I do fail, my
core critical traffic

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:38.670
will fail to the VPN.

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:40.544
STEPHANIE WONG: Right,
as a sort of a backup.

00:15:40.544 --> 00:15:41.380
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yes.

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:43.210
Now, you could advertise
everything over that VPN, too.

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:45.468
But it just sort of depends
how you engineer this.

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:50.720
So let's talk about
advanced routes.

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:53.474
Advanced routes you can really
think of as static routes.

00:15:53.474 --> 00:15:54.890
What you're really
doing is you're

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:57.110
configuring our
SDN control plane

00:15:57.110 --> 00:16:00.020
and telling it how
to route things.

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:03.140
So a typical use case
for this is really

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:05.696
if you're trying to put,
say, a NAT gateway in place.

00:16:05.696 --> 00:16:07.820
Or say you're trying to
run a third party firewall,

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:09.770
and you want to force
traffic to that.

00:16:09.770 --> 00:16:12.500
So a good scenario was, I talked
about earlier, default routes.

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:13.890
So maybe you don't
want to default route

00:16:13.890 --> 00:16:15.681
to Google's internet
gateway, but maybe you

00:16:15.681 --> 00:16:19.040
want the default route to point
to a third party firewall.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:20.840
You would use an advanced
route to do that.

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:23.600
You would basically say, I
want for my default route,

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.360
within this VPC,
the next hop to be

00:16:26.360 --> 00:16:29.505
the IP address of the third
party firewall that I'm using.

00:16:29.505 --> 00:16:30.880
So that's a very
common use case.

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:32.260
But you can sort
of control that.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.843
And I'll take you through some
examples of how that's actually

00:16:34.843 --> 00:16:36.250
used in practice.

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:38.800
When you're actually
configuring an advanced route,

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:41.920
there's really a couple Next
hops that you can specify.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:43.780
1, you can specify
an internet gateway.

00:16:43.780 --> 00:16:45.380
2, a VM instance.

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:46.650
3, an IP address.

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:48.817
Or 4, a VPN tunnel.

00:16:48.817 --> 00:16:51.025
I'll show you how to configure
an advanced route when

00:16:51.025 --> 00:16:51.524
I do a demo.

00:16:51.524 --> 00:16:53.830
STEPHANIE WONG: Perfect.

00:16:53.830 --> 00:16:56.950
RYAN PRZYBYL: So let's
talk about VPC peering.

00:16:56.950 --> 00:17:00.190
VPC peering is one
way in which you can

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:01.889
get two VPCs to talk together.

00:17:01.889 --> 00:17:03.430
The other way is
VPN, which we talked

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:06.619
about a little bit in the
first session that we did.

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:10.150
So the difference with the
VPC peering is it's binary.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:11.980
Whereas when we
talk about VPN, you

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:13.260
can advertise certain subnets.

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:16.000
So if I have a VPN
between VPC A and B,

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:19.950
I could say I only want subnet
A to be advertised across that.

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:21.750
With VPC peering, it's binary.

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:24.690
So all subnets in VPC
A will get advertised

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:27.520
to all subnets in VPC
B. So if you recall,

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:29.370
I talked about
preprogrammed routes

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:30.995
when we first started
the conversation.

00:17:30.995 --> 00:17:32.578
Basically, what
you're doing is taking

00:17:32.578 --> 00:17:34.110
all those preprogrammed
routes on A,

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:35.145
and you're programming
them into B.

00:17:35.145 --> 00:17:36.900
You're taking all the
preprogrammed routes in B

00:17:36.900 --> 00:17:38.941
and programming into A.
That's really what you're

00:17:38.941 --> 00:17:40.435
doing when you do VPC peering.

00:17:40.435 --> 00:17:42.060
Another way to think
about it is you're

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:46.110
sort of smashing two VPCs
together to make one VPC.

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:48.180
Now, the one
fundamental difference

00:17:48.180 --> 00:17:50.230
is security policies.

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:52.560
And we'll talk a bit more
about security and firewalls

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:53.639
in the next session.

00:17:53.639 --> 00:17:55.680
But the key thing to
understand about VPC peering

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:58.630
is the security policies
are done at the VPC level.

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:00.950
So even though you smash
two VPCs together and make

00:18:00.950 --> 00:18:04.500
one logical VPC in terms of
the pre-configured routes,

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:06.990
you still actually have two
separate security policies.

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:08.800
So I've had customers
call me and say, hey,

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:10.140
I've done VPC peering.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:11.970
I've put these
two VPCs together,

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:13.460
but I can't actually
see traffic.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:15.010
Traffic won't route
back and forth.

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:16.800
And typically it's because
you have two separate security

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:18.591
policies, and the
policies are written such

00:18:18.591 --> 00:18:20.970
that you're not allowing
traffic from the other VPC.

00:18:20.970 --> 00:18:23.700
So a lot of times, if
you do VPC peering,

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:26.760
and things aren't working
as you expect them to,

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:28.860
go and check the
firewall policies

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:31.600
and the security policies
that you applied to both VPCs.

00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:33.600
STEPHANIE WONG: This also
makes me kind of think

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:37.152
about can you have overlapping
IP ranges or subnet IP ranges?

00:18:37.152 --> 00:18:37.860
RYAN PRZYBYL: No.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:39.579
So when you peer
two VPCs together,

00:18:39.579 --> 00:18:42.120
you cannot have overlapping IPs,
because it wouldn't know how

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:42.570
to route them.

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:43.300
It would say, hey.

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:44.430
When it has to make
a routing decision,

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:45.800
it says, hey, I
see this IP here,

00:18:45.800 --> 00:18:46.966
and I don't know what to do.

00:18:46.966 --> 00:18:49.045
So actually, if you have
two overlapping subnets,

00:18:49.045 --> 00:18:51.420
and you try to peer two VPCs,
it actually does that check

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:52.860
on the backside
and will tell you

00:18:52.860 --> 00:18:55.140
that you can't peer
those two VPCs together.

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:57.570
So if this is something that
you're planning on using,

00:18:57.570 --> 00:18:59.962
you need to consider that in
terms of your IP allocations

00:18:59.962 --> 00:19:00.920
and your subnet setups.

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:01.630
STEPHANIE WONG: So
when you go to deploy,

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:04.050
it will actually kind of
prohibit you from deploying

00:19:04.050 --> 00:19:04.920
if it realizes that there's--

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:05.680
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:07.680
RYAN PRZYBYL: Correct, so you'll
build two VPCs separately.

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:08.970
And then you'll say,
I want to peer them.

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:10.890
And you have to be
peering on both sides.

00:19:10.890 --> 00:19:12.730
So this can be
used, for example,

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:15.690
to interconnect a VPC
that you own with a VPC

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:18.072
maybe a customer
organization owns.

00:19:18.072 --> 00:19:19.530
So what you don't
want to do is you

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:21.030
don't want to just
be able to enable

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:22.234
VPC peering to another VPC.

00:19:22.234 --> 00:19:24.150
It's sort of like a VPN
configuration in terms

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:25.800
of you've got to configure
the gateway on one side

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:27.030
and the gateway on the other.

00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:28.080
When you do VPC
peering, you have

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:29.160
to configure it on your side.

00:19:29.160 --> 00:19:30.570
And you have to configure
it on the other side.

00:19:30.570 --> 00:19:33.120
And both sides have to be
set up to sort of match.

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:36.720
And then it will allow
the peering to happen.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:40.260
A few things to be
cognisant about,

00:19:40.260 --> 00:19:41.790
there's quotas around this.

00:19:41.790 --> 00:19:45.269
So a common architecture
might be a hub

00:19:45.269 --> 00:19:46.560
and spoke sort of architecture.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:49.080
So you have your hub, and
you have maybe five spokes.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:52.800
As long as the total number of
VMs in that peering arrangement

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:56.850
is not over 15,500, you're OK.

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:58.770
If you go to exceed
15,500, it's not

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:01.010
going to allow you to
setup that peering session.

00:20:01.010 --> 00:20:02.510
The other thing to
understand is you

00:20:02.510 --> 00:20:05.410
can have 25 peered networks.

00:20:05.410 --> 00:20:07.160
So if we have a hub
and spoke environment,

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:08.576
you can think of
I've got one hub.

00:20:08.576 --> 00:20:11.930
I can have 24 spokes as long
as the total number of VMs

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:15.790
are also under 15,500.

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:17.650
So one last thing
to understand there

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:20.650
is the transitive nature, or
non-transitive nature really,

00:20:20.650 --> 00:20:21.970
of peering.

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:24.850
So what I really mean by that
is if you have VPC A, VPC

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:29.260
B, and VPC C, and A is peered
with B, and B is peered with C,

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:31.510
a lot of customers
ask can A get to C.

00:20:31.510 --> 00:20:34.760
And the answer is no, because
it's non transitive in nature.

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:37.420
So if you want A to get to C,
you'd have to peer A and C.

00:20:37.420 --> 00:20:40.450
But A cannot transit
B to get to C.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:43.530
So that's another key
thing to understand.

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:45.280
STEPHANIE WONG: Do you
know the philosophy

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:49.100
behind why Google wanted
to design it that way?

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:51.640
RYAN PRZYBYL: It's
really just to facilitate

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:54.010
interacting with
other VPCs and being

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:56.230
able to take all those
pre-configured routes,

00:20:56.230 --> 00:20:59.032
and being able to
push them other places

00:20:59.032 --> 00:21:00.490
without having to
use a VPN tunnel.

00:21:00.490 --> 00:21:02.590
Like if you just want to say,
I want all the subnets here

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:04.050
to talk to all the subnets
here, but I've built them

00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:05.950
into two separate
VPCs, it allows

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:09.165
you to, in one way, like I
said, merge them into one VPC,

00:21:09.165 --> 00:21:11.290
yet still maintain separate
security policies, too.

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:12.780
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah, so for
that that use case especially?

00:21:12.780 --> 00:21:15.040
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah,
so you may have

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:19.912
a VPC that is sensitive
information that you want

00:21:19.912 --> 00:21:22.120
access, but you want to have
a very specific security

00:21:22.120 --> 00:21:23.027
policy for it.

00:21:23.027 --> 00:21:24.110
That would be one example.

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:26.410
Or like I said, if you're
interfacing with a customer

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:27.040
organization.

00:21:27.040 --> 00:21:30.130
So maybe you're a supplier,
and you have a vendor

00:21:30.130 --> 00:21:31.420
that you interact with.

00:21:31.420 --> 00:21:33.980
Well, you don't want to just
have one VPC with one company

00:21:33.980 --> 00:21:35.040
or the other managing it, right?

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:36.430
You want to have your
own security policy.

00:21:36.430 --> 00:21:38.263
They want to have their
own security policy.

00:21:38.263 --> 00:21:39.294
STEPHANIE WONG: Right.

00:21:39.294 --> 00:21:41.710
RYAN PRZYBYL: So I'm going to
take you through a reference

00:21:41.710 --> 00:21:42.490
architecture now.

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:44.980
This sort of shows how all
of this works together.

00:21:44.980 --> 00:21:47.590
So what I've created
here is three VPCs.

00:21:47.590 --> 00:21:49.800
So I have a standard
VPC on the left.

00:21:49.800 --> 00:21:52.580
I have a shared VPC
in the top middle.

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:54.850
And then I have another
shared VPC in the lower right.

00:21:54.850 --> 00:21:57.310
And then I've put subnets
in there that you can see.

00:21:57.310 --> 00:21:59.890
So 10.1 is in the standard VPC.

00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:03.460
10.0 is actually coming
from an on-prem environment.

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:05.680
10.2 is on the shared
VPC in the middle.

00:22:05.680 --> 00:22:07.630
And 10.3 is in the
shared VPC down

00:22:07.630 --> 00:22:09.320
in the lower right-hand corner.

00:22:09.320 --> 00:22:12.010
So I want to interconnect a
bunch of this stuff together.

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:13.843
So the first thing I'm
going to talk through

00:22:13.843 --> 00:22:16.400
is the standard VPC connecting
it to the on-prem environment.

00:22:16.400 --> 00:22:18.640
So in this case, I'm putting
two Cloud Routers in place,

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:20.060
and I've built
two interconnects.

00:22:20.060 --> 00:22:22.268
So you can see the dedicated
interconnect in the east

00:22:22.268 --> 00:22:23.560
and the central region.

00:22:23.560 --> 00:22:24.940
So what those Cloud
Routers are going to do

00:22:24.940 --> 00:22:26.860
is they're going to learn
all the routes that I have

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:28.380
programmed in the standard VPC.

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:30.640
And they're going to
advertise them via BGP

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:32.080
to the on-prem environment.

00:22:32.080 --> 00:22:34.121
And that's going to allow
the on-prem environment

00:22:34.121 --> 00:22:36.610
to talk to the standard VPC.

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:38.740
So that's the first thing I did.

00:22:38.740 --> 00:22:41.770
The second thing is I
want to peer two of these.

00:22:41.770 --> 00:22:44.230
So if I take the
shared VPC number two,

00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:46.120
I'm going to peer that
with standard VPC.

00:22:46.120 --> 00:22:48.970
So that allows, again,
standard VPC and shared VPC

00:22:48.970 --> 00:22:53.230
to communicate together via
that peering relationship.

00:22:53.230 --> 00:22:56.020
So what I have here
is I have this--

00:22:56.020 --> 00:22:58.420
if you look at the bottom,
it says programmed routes.

00:22:58.420 --> 00:23:01.624
So in standard VPC, I'm going
to now have programmed routes.

00:23:01.624 --> 00:23:03.040
And again, these
are routes that I

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:06.250
talked about in the very first
slide that you can't change.

00:23:06.250 --> 00:23:08.110
Because I set this
peering relationship up,

00:23:08.110 --> 00:23:10.300
I'm going to have
programmed routes for 10.3

00:23:10.300 --> 00:23:12.670
in that standard VPC.

00:23:12.670 --> 00:23:15.100
And in the shared VPC, I'm
going to have programmed

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:19.259
routes for 10.1 subnets or the
10.1.1 and the 10.1.2 subnet,

00:23:19.259 --> 00:23:20.800
because those were
the subnets that I

00:23:20.800 --> 00:23:21.910
created with the standard VPC.

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:23.660
And again, when you
peer the two together,

00:23:23.660 --> 00:23:25.510
it's going to preprogram
all those subnets

00:23:25.510 --> 00:23:26.720
into each of them.

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:30.360
So that's what that
programmed route section is.

00:23:30.360 --> 00:23:32.250
So the shared VPC number 1--

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:34.710
in this case, the padlocks
represent a third party

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:35.994
firewall device.

00:23:35.994 --> 00:23:37.410
So this is a
scenario maybe that's

00:23:37.410 --> 00:23:39.118
a financier VPC or
something, when I want

00:23:39.118 --> 00:23:40.800
to limit connectivity to it.

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.750
So what that device is
doing is it's actually

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:45.060
sitting with one
NIC in standard VPC

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:48.090
and one NIC in the shared VPC.

00:23:48.090 --> 00:23:52.500
So in the standard VPC, I
have the IP of 10.1.1.1.

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:54.660
That's the NIC that's
sitting in the standard VPC.

00:23:54.660 --> 00:23:59.460
And 10.2.1.1 is the NIC that's
sitting in the shared VPC.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:02.500
So now, I want to be able to
route traffic back and forth.

00:24:02.500 --> 00:24:04.420
So I talked about
advanced routes.

00:24:04.420 --> 00:24:07.150
So let's take the
shared VPC number 1.

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:09.150
So in this case, I want
to be able to route

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:10.710
traffic from the
shared VPC number

00:24:10.710 --> 00:24:14.100
1 back to the standard VPC.

00:24:14.100 --> 00:24:17.910
So in this case, I'm putting
an advanced route in there

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:23.370
for 10.0.1, which is really
the subnet in the data center.

00:24:23.370 --> 00:24:26.070
So I'm saying all
of that subnet,

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:27.590
you're telling
shared VPC it exists.

00:24:27.590 --> 00:24:30.090
But it exists somewhere else.

00:24:30.090 --> 00:24:32.850
And the next hop to get
to it is the firewall.

00:24:32.850 --> 00:24:34.780
So that's that 10.2.1.1.

00:24:34.780 --> 00:24:37.860
So what I'm telling
it is for 10.0.1.0,

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:40.470
that whole subnet, if you
need to get there, which

00:24:40.470 --> 00:24:44.310
is the on-prem environment,
go to 10.2.1.1,

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:48.101
which is the NIC that
sits in that VPC.

00:24:48.101 --> 00:24:50.100
I'm also doing the same
thing on the other side.

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:52.440
In the standard
VPC, I'm configuring

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:53.820
another advanced route.

00:24:53.820 --> 00:24:55.830
In this case, the Cloud
Routers are picking up

00:24:55.830 --> 00:24:58.413
this advanced route and they're
advertising it to the on-prem.

00:24:58.413 --> 00:25:03.060
So the on-prem knows I
can get to 10.2 subnet.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:05.220
But when it comes
into the standard VPC,

00:25:05.220 --> 00:25:07.290
it's going to say,
oh, 10.2 doesn't

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:08.610
live in the standard VPC.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:10.470
It lives in the shared VPC.

00:25:10.470 --> 00:25:12.720
And for me to get
to 10.2, I need

00:25:12.720 --> 00:25:18.052
to go to a next hop of 10.1.1.1.

00:25:18.052 --> 00:25:20.010
So this gets a little
complex, but it shows you

00:25:20.010 --> 00:25:22.140
how you can use VPC peering
in terms of the shared

00:25:22.140 --> 00:25:25.080
VPC at the bottom, how you
get preprogrammed routes

00:25:25.080 --> 00:25:26.730
and how they
propagate, but also how

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:30.060
you can inject a third
party firewall in the mix

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:32.310
and then configure advanced
routes to force things

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:33.980
through the firewall, where you
then implement your security

00:25:33.980 --> 00:25:34.480
policy.

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:37.280
STEPHANIE WONG: Right, so
just to reiterate again,

00:25:37.280 --> 00:25:40.600
GCP to GCP peering would be
using preprogrammed routes.

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:42.704
Do peering for
different subnets.

00:25:42.704 --> 00:25:44.370
Meanwhile, you would
use advanced routes

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:46.890
to force traffic to the
next hop, which might exist

00:25:46.890 --> 00:25:48.690
in a third-party firewall.

00:25:48.690 --> 00:25:49.530
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah, it's
usually a third party

00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:50.510
device of some sort.

00:25:50.510 --> 00:25:51.180
So it could be a firewall.

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:53.221
It could be a NAT gateway
that you've configured.

00:25:53.221 --> 00:25:57.070
But you want to force traffic
to a particular device.

00:25:57.070 --> 00:25:58.050
So you're telling it.

00:25:58.050 --> 00:25:59.970
Another example might be--

00:25:59.970 --> 00:26:01.771
let's say that firewall
is the firewall

00:26:01.771 --> 00:26:03.520
that I've used to
connect to the internet.

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:07.230
So I could build a default
route in shared VPC number 1.

00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:11.320
Say for all the zeros, go
to the interface 10.2.1.1.

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:13.680
And that would push anything
that's not part of your 10.

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:15.614
private space to the firewall.

00:26:15.614 --> 00:26:17.280
The firewall would
then do NAT-ing on it

00:26:17.280 --> 00:26:18.720
and be able to push
it, externally,

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:19.070
out to the internet.

00:26:19.070 --> 00:26:19.930
STEPHANIE WONG: OK, understood.

00:26:19.930 --> 00:26:20.520
RYAN PRZYBYL: That's
just another sort

00:26:20.520 --> 00:26:21.910
of common use case for this.

00:26:21.910 --> 00:26:24.100
Like I said, this does
get a little complex.

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:26.492
So it's a little bit deeper
of a topic to understand.

00:26:26.492 --> 00:26:28.950
But it just goes to show that
when you're routing in cloud,

00:26:28.950 --> 00:26:30.860
there are lots of
ways to do this.

00:26:30.860 --> 00:26:31.830
STEPHANIE WONG: And
if they're trying

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:33.538
to map out what they're
doing on premise,

00:26:33.538 --> 00:26:35.730
then the possibilities are
still here in the cloud.

00:26:35.730 --> 00:26:37.440
You can actually kind of
map it out in some way.

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:39.180
RYAN PRZYBYL: Exactly, there's
lots of ways to do this.

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:41.510
Like I said, you can use
VPN tunnels versus VPN

00:26:41.510 --> 00:26:44.184
peering or versus VPC peering.

00:26:44.184 --> 00:26:46.350
Like we talked about, peering
is going to be binary.

00:26:46.350 --> 00:26:48.500
So it's in all subnets
to all subnets--

00:26:48.500 --> 00:26:50.850
versus if you wanted one
subnet to one subnet,

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:53.400
you'd use a VPN tunnel
to connect them, right?

00:26:53.400 --> 00:26:55.890
So again, anything you can
generally do in on-prem,

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:57.980
you can find a way to do
in the cloud environment.

00:26:57.980 --> 00:27:00.060
It's just you have to understand
the complexities of routing

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:01.650
and how it works, and
how a Cloud Router works,

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:02.880
and how routes get
picked up and cascade.

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:04.588
STEPHANIE WONG: How
they differ, as well.

00:27:06.765 --> 00:27:09.480
RYAN PRZYBYL: So I'm going
to take you to a demo

00:27:09.480 --> 00:27:15.360
and show you how this
stuff works in the portal.

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:17.120
So what I've
configured here is I've

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:19.324
configured a demo environment.

00:27:19.324 --> 00:27:21.240
I'll make it a little
bigger so we can see it.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:27.330
So in this demo
environment, I've

00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:33.161
created two VPCs, Cloud OnAir
102 and Cloud OnAir 102 peering

00:27:33.161 --> 00:27:33.660
VPC.

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:37.720
So I'm going to show you how
the VPC peering routes manifest.

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:42.420
And then, in the first VPC, I
have created two subnets, one

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:45.690
in US central, one in US east.

00:27:45.690 --> 00:27:47.600
And that's 10.1 and 10.2.

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:51.662
In the peering VPC, I've
created a subnet 10.3.

00:27:51.662 --> 00:27:52.870
So that's our standard setup.

00:27:52.870 --> 00:27:54.100
Now, I'm going to take
you through the routes,

00:27:54.100 --> 00:27:56.183
so you can actually see
how these routes manifest.

00:27:56.183 --> 00:27:58.740
So when you're looking at
this in the Google portal,

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:01.180
you can understand
what you're looking at.

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:03.100
So I'm going to go over here.

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:09.229
I'm going to go down to routes.

00:28:09.229 --> 00:28:11.520
So this is the first page
when you come into the routes

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:13.080
piece of things.

00:28:13.080 --> 00:28:15.060
It's showing you all
of the routes, here.

00:28:15.060 --> 00:28:17.407
So you can see a number
of these default routes.

00:28:17.407 --> 00:28:18.990
These are the routes
I was telling you

00:28:18.990 --> 00:28:20.820
that are preprogrammed.

00:28:20.820 --> 00:28:24.860
So these first 1, 2, 3, 4, 5--
we've got 5 or six routes here

00:28:24.860 --> 00:28:25.860
that are default routes.

00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:28.322
You can't change these.

00:28:28.322 --> 00:28:29.780
And then it's
showing you we've got

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:31.960
some peering routes in here.

00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:33.960
And then, I actually have
a Cloud Router created

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:37.061
that is actually talking to a
router I've configured off-site

00:28:37.061 --> 00:28:37.560
from this.

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:41.040
And I've set that router to
advertise some subnets down

00:28:41.040 --> 00:28:42.100
here.

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:44.820
So the first page, it
gives you the total view

00:28:44.820 --> 00:28:47.580
of all of your outs, which,
for this simple environment,

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:48.750
is very easy to look at.

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:50.010
But I've seen customers
that literally

00:28:50.010 --> 00:28:50.940
have 10 pages of this.

00:28:50.940 --> 00:28:53.440
So it's a little more
complex to look at.

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:54.990
So if you want to
look at what you're

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:57.040
learning from a
dynamic perspective--

00:28:57.040 --> 00:28:59.400
so you can see these
192.168 blocks,

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:00.840
when I clicked on
the dynamic tab,

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:02.910
this is what I'm learning
from the Cloud Router.

00:29:02.910 --> 00:29:04.761
So again, I configured
a router outside

00:29:04.761 --> 00:29:06.260
of the cloud
environment, and I told

00:29:06.260 --> 00:29:08.590
it to advertise these routes.

00:29:08.590 --> 00:29:10.030
And I didn't put
any MED on there.

00:29:10.030 --> 00:29:12.912
So it's set with a
MED of 0, effectively.

00:29:12.912 --> 00:29:15.120
And that's why it's showing
up as route priority of 0

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:16.350
on this side.

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:17.865
And then the next
top IP address--

00:29:17.865 --> 00:29:20.820
this is actually the IP
address of the off-site router

00:29:20.820 --> 00:29:22.480
that I configured.

00:29:22.480 --> 00:29:24.694
So as I said, when you
look at a VM level,

00:29:24.694 --> 00:29:27.110
you're going to see these
routes get programmed in there--

00:29:27.110 --> 00:29:29.680
those 192.168 routes.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:32.070
And if they had to route,
they would build an IP packet

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:36.330
with the destination
of 169.254.40.50.

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:39.060
And it would route directly
to that off-prem router

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:39.935
that I've configured.

00:29:42.750 --> 00:29:45.060
The other part is if you
can click on peering,

00:29:45.060 --> 00:29:47.143
this is going to show you
all of the routes you're

00:29:47.143 --> 00:29:48.450
learning via VPC peering.

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:52.102
So again, you can look at all
the routes in this first tab,

00:29:52.102 --> 00:29:54.060
but it's going to get
very big and very complex

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:56.230
if you're building any
sort of real environment.

00:29:56.230 --> 00:29:59.020
So sometimes, it's easier to
look at your default routes,

00:29:59.020 --> 00:29:59.520
here.

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:01.500
But again, you
can't change those.

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:03.250
And then if you want
to look at the routes

00:30:03.250 --> 00:30:05.440
that you're learning, you
click on the dynamic tab.

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:07.231
If you want to look at
your peering routes,

00:30:07.231 --> 00:30:08.640
you'd click on the peering tab.

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:12.260
So that's how your
routes show up in here.

00:30:12.260 --> 00:30:15.540
So create route-- this is
basically the advanced route

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:16.990
function I was talking about.

00:30:16.990 --> 00:30:18.600
So if I click on
that, it's going

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:20.950
to take me into the ability
to create the route.

00:30:20.950 --> 00:30:23.120
So I'm going to give
the route a name.

00:30:23.120 --> 00:30:25.630
I'm going to tell it what
network I want it to live in.

00:30:25.630 --> 00:30:28.450
I'm going to prescribe an IP
range for it, a simple route

00:30:28.450 --> 00:30:31.867
priority, just like I do
for all normal routes.

00:30:31.867 --> 00:30:33.700
And then, as I was
saying, you can basically

00:30:33.700 --> 00:30:34.990
point it to different things.

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:37.060
So this is like saying if you're
going to point to a firewall--

00:30:37.060 --> 00:30:38.768
if you're going to
point to a VPN tunnel,

00:30:38.768 --> 00:30:41.440
you would select what you
want to point that route to.

00:30:41.440 --> 00:30:43.270
So that's how you
configure it here.

00:30:43.270 --> 00:30:44.010
And then you just click Create.

00:30:44.010 --> 00:30:45.230
It'll create that route.

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:47.710
So in that VPC, that
route will then live.

00:30:47.710 --> 00:30:49.940
And all the VMs will get
programmed with that route.

00:30:49.940 --> 00:30:51.520
So like I said, it could
be a default route.

00:30:51.520 --> 00:30:53.103
It could be a lot
of different things.

00:30:53.103 --> 00:30:55.960
STEPHANIE WONG: Can you do all
of this in the Cloud Shell or--

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:57.186
RYAN PRZYBYL: You can.

00:30:57.186 --> 00:30:58.810
I could do this in
Cloud Shell, but you

00:30:58.810 --> 00:31:01.060
wouldn't want to sit here and
watch me type everything out.

00:31:01.060 --> 00:31:03.080
Sometimes, it's easier
to do it in the console.

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:04.455
STEPHANIE WONG:
Yeah, absolutely.

00:31:04.455 --> 00:31:06.580
RYAN PRZYBYL: So lastly,
I want to show how easy it

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:08.332
is to configure a Cloud Router.

00:31:08.332 --> 00:31:09.790
Because they are
very, very simple.

00:31:09.790 --> 00:31:11.190
People think of
configuring a router--

00:31:11.190 --> 00:31:12.565
they think I'm
going to configure

00:31:12.565 --> 00:31:15.490
my Cisco or Juniper, which can
be a very complex thing to do.

00:31:15.490 --> 00:31:18.640
In this case, it's
actually very simple.

00:31:18.640 --> 00:31:20.860
So this is the Cloud Router
I've actually stood up.

00:31:20.860 --> 00:31:22.480
As I said, I have a Cloud
Router in this environment.

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:25.021
I'm learning routes from another
router that I've configured.

00:31:25.021 --> 00:31:27.395
So you can see I've got an
up BGP session right here.

00:31:27.395 --> 00:31:30.020
But what I'm going to do is I'm
going to create another router.

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:32.545
So in this case, let's
just give it a name.

00:31:32.545 --> 00:31:36.030
We'll just call it Test 1.

00:31:36.030 --> 00:31:38.740
I have to drop it
in a particular VPC.

00:31:38.740 --> 00:31:40.480
I could drop it in
a region as I said.

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:41.938
If you're using
regional or global,

00:31:41.938 --> 00:31:43.250
this could be important.

00:31:43.250 --> 00:31:46.610
So I'm going to drop
this in US central 1.

00:31:46.610 --> 00:31:48.490
And then I have to tell
it what ASN number is

00:31:48.490 --> 00:31:49.240
this representing.

00:31:49.240 --> 00:31:53.350
So the cloud order I configured
in here before was 65001.

00:31:53.350 --> 00:31:56.130
So to be consistent, I'm going
to put the same thing in here.

00:31:56.130 --> 00:31:58.870
I'm going to click Create.

00:31:58.870 --> 00:32:02.670
And it's going to go, and it's
going to create a Cloud Router.

00:32:02.670 --> 00:32:03.819
So this is a cloud.

00:32:03.819 --> 00:32:06.360
It's literally that simple to
create the actual Cloud Router.

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:07.770
Now, I have to
attach it to things.

00:32:07.770 --> 00:32:09.190
So I have to attach
it to a VPN, or I

00:32:09.190 --> 00:32:10.689
have to attach it
to an interconnect

00:32:10.689 --> 00:32:12.030
or various other things.

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:13.542
So that would be
the next step here.

00:32:13.542 --> 00:32:15.000
And then once you
have it attached,

00:32:15.000 --> 00:32:16.770
then you're going
to bring BGP up.

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:17.860
So then you can start
advertising routes.

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:19.151
So really, there's three steps.

00:32:19.151 --> 00:32:21.360
There's creating Cloud
Router, attaching it

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:23.520
to something like a
tunnel or an interconnect,

00:32:23.520 --> 00:32:25.650
and then establishing
your BGP connectivity

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:27.490
and then going back to the
routes menu that I showed you

00:32:27.490 --> 00:32:29.050
and verifying that your
routes are flowing.

00:32:29.050 --> 00:32:31.170
STEPHANIE WONG: So you might
have some configuration

00:32:31.170 --> 00:32:33.200
that you need to do back on
your on premise network--

00:32:33.200 --> 00:32:34.150
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah, you're
going to have to configure

00:32:34.150 --> 00:32:35.190
your on-prem router.

00:32:35.190 --> 00:32:38.040
So if you set this up to,
say, connect to a VPN tunnel,

00:32:38.040 --> 00:32:40.260
and that VPN tunnel
terminates on-prem,

00:32:40.260 --> 00:32:41.670
you're going to have to
configure your Cloud Router.

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:42.810
You're going to configure
your VPN gateway.

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:44.970
You're going to reconfigure your
on-prem router, and your VPN

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:46.890
gateway, and your
on-prem environment,

00:32:46.890 --> 00:32:48.200
just like you would in
the cloud environment.

00:32:48.200 --> 00:32:49.710
Once everything is
configured on both sides,

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:52.240
then you'll see the BGP session
come up like I have here.

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:55.020
This green check is
what you're looking for.

00:32:55.020 --> 00:32:55.844
So that's it.

00:32:55.844 --> 00:32:57.510
That's pretty much a
high level overview

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:00.570
of routing in GCP
and VPC peering.

00:33:00.570 --> 00:33:01.934
So let's go take some questions.

00:33:01.934 --> 00:33:03.850
STEPHANIE WONG: We'll
be back in just a minute

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:05.235
for some questions.

00:33:55.954 --> 00:33:58.120
All right, thanks so much
for all of your questions.

00:33:58.120 --> 00:33:59.120
Here we have a handful.

00:33:59.120 --> 00:34:00.453
I'll go ahead and read them out.

00:34:00.453 --> 00:34:02.720
The first one is, is the
Cloud Router operating

00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:04.585
and, or, configurable
at layer 2 or 3?

00:34:04.585 --> 00:34:05.960
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
the Cloud Router

00:34:05.960 --> 00:34:07.280
itself is a layer 3 device.

00:34:07.280 --> 00:34:09.409
It operates just like
a traditional router.

00:34:09.409 --> 00:34:11.007
Depending on the
interconnect type--

00:34:11.007 --> 00:34:13.340
so if you're using a dedicated
interconnect or a partner

00:34:13.340 --> 00:34:15.929
interconnect, that's really
a layer 2 interconnect.

00:34:15.929 --> 00:34:18.290
We are going to configure
the interconnect.

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:18.870
And then, what
you're going to do

00:34:18.870 --> 00:34:20.661
is you're going to
build a VLAN attachment.

00:34:20.661 --> 00:34:22.020
So that's your layer 2 element.

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:23.989
And what you do is
you attach the VLAN

00:34:23.989 --> 00:34:26.655
off of the interconnect
to the Cloud Router.

00:34:26.655 --> 00:34:29.030
But the actual configuring,
in terms of the Cloud Router,

00:34:29.030 --> 00:34:31.297
it's actually operating
only at a layer 3 level.

00:34:31.297 --> 00:34:32.880
It's really just an
IP routing device.

00:34:32.880 --> 00:34:35.120
Like I said, it's
really a BGP speaker.

00:34:35.120 --> 00:34:36.350
That's really what it is.

00:34:36.350 --> 00:34:38.600
STEPHANIE WONG: Great, so
next question is, can you

00:34:38.600 --> 00:34:41.179
apply instance and route
tags to route traffic

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:42.689
to specific instances?

00:34:42.689 --> 00:34:43.730
RYAN PRZYBYL: So you can.

00:34:43.730 --> 00:34:47.230
So there's a concept of
tags, where you can apply--

00:34:47.230 --> 00:34:48.230
so say I create a route.

00:34:48.230 --> 00:34:51.404
And I want to route to only
be applied to certain VMs.

00:34:51.404 --> 00:34:53.070
When you create the
route you could say,

00:34:53.070 --> 00:34:55.460
I only want this route
to live on certain VMs.

00:34:55.460 --> 00:34:57.740
And you would do that
by specifying a tag.

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:00.090
So you could say,
I want this route.

00:35:00.090 --> 00:35:01.750
So maybe it's a
default route you

00:35:01.750 --> 00:35:06.122
don't want to live everywhere
or some other specific route.

00:35:06.122 --> 00:35:07.580
You would build
that route, and you

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:10.896
would say I want it to live on
VMs that have the right tag.

00:35:10.896 --> 00:35:13.020
And then when you create
the VMs, you create the VM

00:35:13.020 --> 00:35:14.504
and put the red tag on it.

00:35:14.504 --> 00:35:16.670
And then what will happen
is it'll import that route

00:35:16.670 --> 00:35:17.990
into that particular VM.

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:20.790
So you could have routes that
live on some VMs and not others

00:35:20.790 --> 00:35:22.370
by using tags.

00:35:22.370 --> 00:35:25.112
STEPHANIE WONG: So you
can map using tags.

00:35:25.112 --> 00:35:26.570
Next question is,
how do you ensure

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:28.310
you can maintain
routing locality

00:35:28.310 --> 00:35:30.716
to keep the next hop from
the same region or zone?

00:35:30.716 --> 00:35:32.850
RYAN PRZYBYL: So
routing locality--

00:35:32.850 --> 00:35:35.879
it's sort of a complex
piece of the puzzle.

00:35:35.879 --> 00:35:38.170
So this is like where is your
interconnect versus where

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:38.878
are your subnets.

00:35:38.878 --> 00:35:40.430
This gets into
the topic of where

00:35:40.430 --> 00:35:42.830
do you put your ILBs
and things like that.

00:35:42.830 --> 00:35:44.930
So it's really about
understanding where you've

00:35:44.930 --> 00:35:50.330
provisioned things and then
setting up the routes to route

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:51.440
appropriately to them.

00:35:51.440 --> 00:35:54.650
So for example, if I have
an interconnect in the east,

00:35:54.650 --> 00:35:57.050
and I only want the
subnets in my east region

00:35:57.050 --> 00:35:58.670
to flow through
that interconnect,

00:35:58.670 --> 00:36:00.350
I probably wouldn't
use global routing.

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:02.069
I might just use
regional routing

00:36:02.069 --> 00:36:04.360
and only advertise the subnets
east of the interconnect

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:04.900
in the east.

00:36:04.900 --> 00:36:06.650
And maybe I do the
same thing in the west.

00:36:06.650 --> 00:36:08.899
I have a Cloud Router in the
west with an interconnect

00:36:08.899 --> 00:36:10.460
in the west, and
then I only want

00:36:10.460 --> 00:36:13.490
those routes to be advertised
to that interconnect.

00:36:13.490 --> 00:36:15.467
So that what you're
maintaining locality

00:36:15.467 --> 00:36:17.550
where the interconnect is
in the same region where

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:19.841
the subnets are, versus if
you're using global routing,

00:36:19.841 --> 00:36:20.960
like I explained before.

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:24.170
You could actually advertise
your east subnets to the west.

00:36:24.170 --> 00:36:26.540
But they're going have
an inflated MED, because

00:36:26.540 --> 00:36:29.610
of the RTT distance that we're
going to compute for you.

00:36:29.610 --> 00:36:32.670
So there are ways to do it.

00:36:32.670 --> 00:36:36.062
A lot of times, like I
said, the biggest thing

00:36:36.062 --> 00:36:37.520
you have to be
concerned about here

00:36:37.520 --> 00:36:39.691
is when you start to
put ILBs in the mix.

00:36:39.691 --> 00:36:41.690
Because then global routing
can really bite you.

00:36:41.690 --> 00:36:43.190
You want to make
sure you understand

00:36:43.190 --> 00:36:44.540
the concept of locality.

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:46.920
So where the
interconnect's coming in,

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:48.740
the ILB has to be
in the same region.

00:36:48.740 --> 00:36:50.760
If you have an
interconnect in the west

00:36:50.760 --> 00:36:52.772
and an ILB in the
central region,

00:36:52.772 --> 00:36:54.230
the interconnect
lives in the west.

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:55.570
And the ILB lives in
the central region.

00:36:55.570 --> 00:36:57.140
And they can't
talk to each other.

00:36:57.140 --> 00:36:58.848
So if you have global
routing, and you're

00:36:58.848 --> 00:37:00.410
advertising a subnet
on the central

00:37:00.410 --> 00:37:02.090
through the interconnect
in the west,

00:37:02.090 --> 00:37:03.080
it's actually going
to come in the west.

00:37:03.080 --> 00:37:04.534
But it can't
actually hit the ILB.

00:37:04.534 --> 00:37:05.450
STEPHANIE WONG: I see.

00:37:05.450 --> 00:37:07.745
RYAN PRZYBYL: So that's
where this gets more complex.

00:37:07.745 --> 00:37:08.900
STEPHANIE WONG: So there's
three factors-- load balancers,

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:10.700
keeping it as a
regional Cloud Router,

00:37:10.700 --> 00:37:12.770
and advertising the
appropriate subnets.

00:37:12.770 --> 00:37:14.610
RYAN PRZYBYL: Yeah, how you
want to advertise the subnets.

00:37:14.610 --> 00:37:17.150
STEPHANIE WONG: Great, next
question is, are firewall rules

00:37:17.150 --> 00:37:18.560
imported into a peered network?

00:37:18.560 --> 00:37:19.550
RYAN PRZYBYL: So no.

00:37:19.550 --> 00:37:21.680
So when you do VPC
peering, as I mentioned,

00:37:21.680 --> 00:37:24.020
the firewall rules
and the policies

00:37:24.020 --> 00:37:25.250
are designed separately.

00:37:25.250 --> 00:37:28.250
So VPC A has its own set of
security policies and rules.

00:37:28.250 --> 00:37:30.950
VPC has its own
policies and rules.

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:35.600
So let's say, for example, I
have 10.1 in VPC A and 10.2

00:37:35.600 --> 00:37:40.010
in VPC B. I may have firewall
rules that allow 10.2 to talk

00:37:40.010 --> 00:37:41.510
to a whole bunch of stuff.

00:37:41.510 --> 00:37:46.640
And I may have firewall rules in
the VPC A that lock down 10.1.

00:37:46.640 --> 00:37:50.570
Those rules I've written do
not go across with peering.

00:37:50.570 --> 00:37:52.970
So you still have to configure
the security policies

00:37:52.970 --> 00:37:54.560
independently.

00:37:54.560 --> 00:37:57.357
And you have to enable that
peering flow, potentially.

00:37:57.357 --> 00:37:58.940
It depends what your
policy really is.

00:37:58.940 --> 00:38:00.740
If you have your policy
locked down really tight,

00:38:00.740 --> 00:38:03.000
you could peer two things, and
actually, nothing could happen.

00:38:03.000 --> 00:38:04.583
If you have a very
loose policy, where

00:38:04.583 --> 00:38:07.300
you're sort of letting any
VM talk to any VM with, say,

00:38:07.300 --> 00:38:09.530
a 10. address, then
chances are you're

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:10.940
not going to have any issues.

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:11.920
STEPHANIE WONG: It
depends if they clash.

00:38:11.920 --> 00:38:13.620
RYAN PRZYBYL: But to answer
the direct question, no.

00:38:13.620 --> 00:38:16.350
The rules that you write in
policy A when you do peering

00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:18.652
do not go over to VPC B.

00:38:18.652 --> 00:38:19.610
STEPHANIE WONG: Got it.

00:38:19.610 --> 00:38:21.200
And the last one
is, can an instance

00:38:21.200 --> 00:38:25.220
have more than one network
interface with each interface

00:38:25.220 --> 00:38:27.000
in different VPC networks?

00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:28.310
Sorry, say that again.

00:38:28.310 --> 00:38:29.580
RYAN PRZYBYL: So this
was a good example.

00:38:29.580 --> 00:38:30.955
This was the third
party firewall

00:38:30.955 --> 00:38:33.510
that I configured
in that use-case.

00:38:33.510 --> 00:38:36.320
So that was an underlying VM
running a third party firewall

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:37.310
appliance on top of it.

00:38:37.310 --> 00:38:39.327
And it had one NIC
sitting in the VPC

00:38:39.327 --> 00:38:41.660
on the left and one NIC sitting
in the VPC on the right.

00:38:41.660 --> 00:38:42.909
So you can absolutely do that.

00:38:42.909 --> 00:38:44.910
You actually have eight
NICs to play with.

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:49.100
Now, one NIC can
only sit in one VPC.

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:51.110
So if I have a VM, and
there's eight NICs on it,

00:38:51.110 --> 00:38:53.510
only one of those NICs can live
in VPC A. One of those NICs

00:38:53.510 --> 00:38:55.400
can live in VPC B. I
can basically connect it

00:38:55.400 --> 00:38:56.640
to eight different VPCs.

00:38:56.640 --> 00:38:58.130
STEPHANIE WONG: And
that's the instance level.

00:38:58.130 --> 00:38:59.910
But one VM could have
multiple networking--

00:38:59.910 --> 00:39:00.000
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:01.583
